---
/**
 * Terms of service: website terms (Part A) and terms of business for paid services (Part B).
 *
 * Drafted for a Northern Ireland sole trader selling mainly to businesses, against:
 * Provision of Services Regulations 2009 (regs 7, 8, 11, 12); Electronic Commerce (EC Directive)
 * Regulations 2002 (regs 6 and 9); Consumer Rights Act 2015 (ss. 49 to 57 and Part 2);
 * Consumer Contracts (Information, Cancellation and Additional Charges) Regulations 2013;
 * Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act 2024 (s. 308 ADR notice, banned fake reviews);
 * Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977 (ss. 2, 3, 11); Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest)
 * Act 1998; Contracts (Rights of Third Parties) Act 1999; UK GDPR Article 28.
 *
 * Defaults marked "unless your proposal says otherwise" are deliberate: the written proposal
 * for each job can vary them, and takes priority (clause 6.3).
 */
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro';
import { business } from '../config/business';

const updated = '7 November 2026';
const sections = [
  { id: 'summary', label: 'Summary' },
  { id: 'about-these-terms', label: '1. About these terms' },
  { id: 'about-us', label: '2. About us' },
  { id: 'website', label: '3. Using this website' },
  { id: 'audit', label: '4. The free audit' },
  { id: 'customers', label: '5. Business and consumer customers' },
  { id: 'contract', label: '6. How a contract is made' },
  { id: 'services', label: '7. Our services' },
  { id: 'your-responsibilities', label: '8. Your responsibilities' },
  { id: 'changes', label: '9. Changes to the work' },
  { id: 'fees', label: '10. Fees and payment' },
  { id: 'ongoing', label: '11. Ongoing support' },
  { id: 'cancellation', label: '12. Consumer cancellation rights' },
  { id: 'ownership', label: '13. Ownership of the work' },
  { id: 'confidentiality', label: '14. Confidentiality' },
  { id: 'data', label: '15. Data protection' },
  { id: 'accounts', label: '16. Your accounts and platforms' },
  { id: 'reviews', label: '17. Reviews and listings' },
  { id: 'liability', label: '18. Our liability' },
  { id: 'ending', label: '19. Ending a contract' },
  { id: 'outside-control', label: '20. Events outside our control' },
  { id: 'complaints', label: '21. Complaints and disputes' },
  { id: 'general', label: '22. General' },
  { id: 'data-processing', label: 'Schedule 1: Data processing' },
  { id: 'cancellation-form', label: 'Schedule 2: Cancellation form' },
];
const owner = business.legalName || 'the owner';
const phoneHref = business.telephone.replace(/[^\d+]/g, '');
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Terms of service"
  description="The terms for using this website and the free audit, and the terms of business that apply when you hire Presence Web Services for paid work."
  sections={sections}
  updated={updated}
>
  <section id="summary" class="legal-summary" aria-labelledby="summary-heading">
    <h2 id="summary-heading">Summary</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Using this website and requesting a free audit doesn’t commit you to anything or create a
        contract.
      </li>
      <li>
        Paid work only starts after you accept a written proposal. The proposal sets out the work
        and price, and these terms fill in the rest.
      </li>
      <li>
        You keep ownership of your website, domain and Google listing, and you own the work we
        create for you once it is paid for.
      </li>
      <li>We can’t guarantee rankings, enquiries or sales, and we never sell fake reviews.</li>
      <li>
        If you are a consumer, you have legal rights, including a 14-day right to cancel in most
        cases, that nothing in these terms takes away.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <h2 class="legal-part">Part A: This website and the free audit</h2>

  <section id="about-these-terms">
    <h2>1. About these terms</h2>
    <p>
      1.1 These terms apply when you use this website, when you request a free audit, and, as set
      out in Part B, when you hire us for paid work. Please read them carefully. By using this
      website you agree to Part A. Part B applies when you accept a proposal from us.
    </p>
    <p>
      1.2 Our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains how we use personal information and
      forms part of these terms.
    </p>
    <p>
      1.3 These terms are available in English only. You can print or save this page, and we will
      email you a copy of the version that applies to your contract if you ask.
    </p>
    <p>
      1.4 We may update these terms from time to time, and the date at the top shows when they last
      changed. A change will not affect a contract you have already entered into unless you agree to
      it, except as clause 11.3 allows for ongoing support.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="about-us">
    <h2>2. About us</h2>
    <p>
      2.1 Presence Web Services is the trading name of {owner}, a sole trader. “We”, “us” and “our”
      mean {owner} trading as Presence Web Services. “You” and “your” mean the person or business
      using this website or hiring us.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {business.correspondenceAddress && <li>Address: {business.correspondenceAddress}</li>}
      <li>
        Email: <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      </li>
      {business.telephone && (
        <li>
          Telephone: <a href={`tel:${phoneHref}`}>{business.telephone}</a>
        </li>
      )}
    </ul>
    <p>
      2.2 We are not registered for VAT, so no VAT is added to our prices. If we register for VAT in
      future, we will tell you in advance; prices in a proposal you have already accepted will not
      increase as a result. We are not registered in a public trade register, are not a member of a
      regulated profession, and do not need any licence or authorisation to provide our services.
    </p>
    <p>
      2.3 We don’t offer any guarantee or after-sales warranty beyond those given in these terms and
      by law.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="website">
    <h2>3. Using this website</h2>
    <p>
      3.1 You may use this website for lawful purposes only. You must not misuse it, including by
      trying to gain unauthorised access to it or the systems it runs on, introducing viruses or
      malicious code, sending spam or automated requests, or submitting information that is false,
      unlawful, defamatory or that you have no right to share.
    </p>
    <p>
      3.2 The content of this website is for general information only. It is not professional advice
      for your particular situation, and you should not rely on it as such. We take care to keep it
      accurate, but we can’t promise that it is complete or always up to date.
    </p>
    <p>
      3.3 We may change, suspend or withdraw the website at any time, and we don’t guarantee that it
      will always be available or free from errors.
    </p>
    <p>
      3.4 The website and its content, including the text, design, graphics and the Presence Web
      Services name and logo, belong to us or are used under licence. You may view, print and share
      pages for your own non-commercial use, but you may not copy or reuse them for any other
      purpose without our written permission.
    </p>
    <p>
      3.5 Links to other websites are provided for convenience. We have no control over those
      websites and are not responsible for their content or for any loss caused by using them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="audit">
    <h2>4. Enquiries and the free audit</h2>
    <p>
      4.1 Sending an enquiry or requesting an audit does not create a contract and does not commit
      you to buy anything. Nothing on this website is an offer that you can accept to form a
      contract. Contracts are only made as clause 6 describes.
    </p>
    <p>
      4.2 The free audit is a review of the information about your business that is publicly
      available online, such as your website and Google Business Profile. We look at how clearly
      your services are explained, whether your business details are accurate and consistent, and
      how easy it is for customers to contact you. We then send you our findings and recommended
      priorities.
    </p>
    <p>
      4.3 The audit is our honest professional opinion based on what we can see at the time. It does
      not include access to your private systems or accounts, security or penetration testing, or a
      complete technical, legal, accessibility or data protection compliance review, and it should
      not be relied on as one.
    </p>
    <p>
      4.4 The audit is free and you are under no obligation to act on it or to hire us. You may use
      the findings we send you however you like, including with another provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      4.5 We may decline an audit request, for example if we can’t find enough public information or
      the request falls outside what we do, and we don’t promise to complete an audit within a
      particular time. We will let you know if we can’t help.
    </p>
    <p>4.6 You must only ask us to audit a business you own or are authorised to act for.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="customers">
    <h2>5. Business and consumer customers</h2>
    <p>
      5.1 Our services are designed for businesses. You are a <strong>business customer</strong> if
      you hire us for purposes relating to your trade, business, craft or profession, including as a
      sole trader. You are a <strong>consumer</strong> if you are an individual hiring us wholly or
      mainly for purposes outside any trade, business, craft or profession.
    </p>
    <p>
      5.2 Some parts of these terms apply differently to consumers, and those parts say so. If you
      are a consumer, you have legal rights in relation to services that are not performed with
      reasonable care and skill, or that don’t match what we told you. Nothing in these terms
      affects those rights. Free advice about your rights is available from Consumerline (see{' '}
      <a
        href="https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/about-consumerline-and-how-contact-helpline"
        rel="external"
      >
        nidirect
      </a>
      ) or Citizens Advice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2 class="legal-part">Part B: Terms of business for paid work</h2>

  <section id="contract">
    <h2>6. How a contract is made</h2>
    <p>
      6.1 Before any paid work, we will send you a written proposal, usually by email. It will set
      out the work we will do, what you will receive, the price, how and when you pay, the expected
      timing, what we need from you and, for ongoing support, how either of us can end it.
    </p>
    <p>
      6.2 A contract is formed when you accept the proposal in writing, for example by replying to
      our email to say so, and we confirm it. Contracts are made by exchange of email; there is no
      online checkout. If you notice a mistake in a proposal, tell us before you accept it and we
      will correct it.
    </p>
    <p>
      6.3 Your contract is made up of the accepted proposal and these terms (the version shown on
      this page on the date you accept). If the proposal and these terms conflict, the proposal
      takes priority. Unless you are a consumer, these documents are the whole agreement between us
      about the work. This does not limit any liability for fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation.
    </p>
    <p>
      6.4 A proposal is valid for 30 days unless it says otherwise. After that, we may need to
      review the price or timing before you accept.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="services">
    <h2>7. Our services</h2>
    <p>
      7.1 We will carry out the work described in your accepted proposal with reasonable care and
      skill and in line with good industry practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      7.2 Timescales in a proposal are our best estimate. We will keep you informed and tell you
      promptly about anything likely to cause a delay. Where we depend on something from you, such
      as access, content or approval, the timescale will move accordingly.
    </p>
    <p>
      7.3 We will not guarantee search rankings, website traffic, enquiries, bookings, sales or any
      other business result. These depend on many things outside our control, including Google and
      other platforms, your competitors and your customers. Any figures we report are based on the
      information available and don’t on their own prove that a particular change caused a
      particular result.
    </p>
    <p>
      7.4 We may use AI-assisted tools to help us analyse, draft, write code and check our work. We
      review everything ourselves and remain responsible for it. We won’t enter your confidential
      information or personal data into those tools unless you agree.
    </p>
    <p>
      7.5 We will only subcontract any part of the work with your prior agreement. We remain
      responsible for work done by a subcontractor.
    </p>
    <p>
      7.6 We don’t provide large custom software development, paid advertising management or
      enterprise SEO campaigns, or legal, accounting or security consultancy. Anything not described
      in your proposal is not included.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="your-responsibilities">
    <h2>8. Your responsibilities</h2>
    <p>8.1 You agree to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        give us accurate, complete information and the access, content and decisions we reasonably
        need, when we need them;
      </li>
      <li>
        review and approve changes to your business information, and check that anything we publish
        on your behalf is accurate before it goes live;
      </li>
      <li>
        make sure you own, or have permission to use, all content, images, logos and other material
        you give us, and that using it won’t break the law or anyone else’s rights;
      </li>
      <li>
        keep your own accounts, domain, hosting and subscriptions in good standing and paid for,
        unless your proposal says we will manage a particular cost;
      </li>
      <li>
        keep your own backups and copies of your website and content, even where we also take
        backups as part of the work; and
      </li>
      <li>comply with the terms of the platforms your business uses, including Google.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      8.2 If you don’t do something you are responsible for, we won’t be responsible for any
      resulting delay or problem, and we may charge for extra work it causes, after telling you.
    </p>
    <p>
      8.3 If you are a business customer, you will compensate us for any claim brought against us by
      a third party because material you supplied infringed their rights, provided we tell you
      promptly about the claim and let you handle it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="changes">
    <h2>9. Changes to the work</h2>
    <p>
      9.1 Either of us can ask to change the work. If a change affects the price or timing, we will
      tell you and only go ahead once you have agreed in writing, which can be by email.
    </p>
    <p>
      9.2 We may make minor changes needed to comply with the law, or to deal with changes to a
      platform you use, that don’t materially affect the work or the price. We will tell you about
      them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="fees">
    <h2>10. Fees and payment</h2>
    <p>
      10.1 Prices on this website are starting prices. The price for your work is the one in your
      accepted proposal. We are not registered for VAT, so no VAT is added.
    </p>
    <p>
      10.2 Third-party costs, such as domains, hosting, paid plugins, themes, stock images, software
      subscriptions and advertising, are not included unless your proposal says so. We will not
      commit you to any such cost without your agreement.
    </p>
    <p>
      10.3 Unless your proposal says otherwise, we will invoice one-off work on completion (or in
      the instalments set out in the proposal), and ongoing support monthly in advance. Invoices are
      payable within 14 days by bank transfer to the account shown on the invoice.
    </p>
    <p>
      10.4 If you think an invoice is wrong, please tell us promptly and pay any part that is not in
      dispute. We will work with you to resolve it.
    </p>
    <p>
      10.5 If you are a business customer and pay late, we may charge statutory interest and
      compensation under the Late Payment of Commercial Debts (Interest) Act 1998. If an invoice is
      more than 14 days overdue, we may also pause work after giving you at least seven days’
      written notice, until it is paid.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ongoing">
    <h2>11. Ongoing support</h2>
    <p>
      11.1 Ongoing support covers the checks, updates and improvements described in your proposal,
      with priorities agreed with you as we go. It is not unlimited work: anything substantial
      outside its scope, such as a new website or a major rebuild, will be quoted separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      11.2 Ongoing support has no minimum term unless your proposal sets one. Either of us can end
      it by giving at least 30 days’ written notice, which can be by email. You pay for support up
      to the end of the notice period.
    </p>
    <p>
      11.3 We may change the monthly price by giving you at least 30 days’ written notice. If you
      don’t want to continue at the new price, you can end the support before it takes effect
      without paying the increase.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="cancellation">
    <h2>12. Consumer cancellation rights</h2>
    <p>
      12.1 This clause applies only if you are a consumer and the contract was made at a distance,
      for example by email or phone, or away from our business premises. Business customers do not
      have this right.
    </p>
    <p>
      12.2 You have the right to cancel the contract within 14 days without giving any reason. The
      cancellation period ends 14 days after the day the contract is made.
    </p>
    <p>
      12.3 To cancel, tell us clearly in writing before the period ends, by email to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      {business.correspondenceAddress && ` or by post to ${business.correspondenceAddress}`}. You
      can use the <a href="#cancellation-form">model cancellation form</a> in Schedule 2, but you
      don’t have to.
    </p>
    <p>
      12.4 If you cancel, we will refund all payments you have made within 14 days of being told you
      have cancelled, using the same payment method unless you agree otherwise. There is no fee for
      the refund.
    </p>
    <p>
      12.5 We will not start work during the cancellation period unless you expressly ask us to in
      writing, which can be by email. If you ask us to start and then cancel, you must pay for the
      work done up to the time you tell us you are cancelling, in proportion to the full service.
    </p>
    <p>
      12.6 If you ask us to start work during the cancellation period, and acknowledge that you will
      lose the right to cancel once the work is complete, you will lose that right once we have
      fully performed the service.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="ownership">
    <h2>13. Ownership of the work</h2>
    <p>
      13.1 You keep ownership of everything you already own, including your website, domain, Google
      Business Profile, accounts and content. You give us a licence to use your material only as
      needed to do the work.
    </p>
    <p>
      13.2 Once you have paid in full for the work, you own the copyright and other intellectual
      property rights in the designs, text, code and other deliverables we create specifically for
      you.
    </p>
    <p>
      13.3 We keep ownership of our own tools, templates, methods, know-how and any general material
      we created before or separately from your work. Where any of it forms part of your
      deliverables, we give you a permanent, non-exclusive, royalty-free licence to use it as part
      of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      13.4 Some deliverables may include third-party components, such as fonts, plugins, themes,
      images or open-source software. These are provided under their own licence terms, which we
      will tell you about where they affect you.
    </p>
    <p>
      13.5 We will only use your name, logo or work as an example in our marketing, such as a case
      study, with your written permission.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="confidentiality">
    <h2>14. Confidentiality</h2>
    <p>
      14.1 Each of us will keep the other’s confidential information private and use it only to
      perform or receive the services. This does not apply to information that is already public
      (other than through a breach of this clause), that the other party already lawfully had, or
      that must be disclosed by law or to professional advisers bound by confidentiality.
    </p>
    <p>14.2 This clause continues after the contract ends.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>15. Data protection</h2>
    <p>
      15.1 Each of us will comply with UK data protection law. Our{' '}
      <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> explains how we handle personal information as a
      controller.
    </p>
    <p>
      15.2 Where we process personal information on your behalf as part of the work, for example
      messages sent through your website’s contact form or details in your Google Business Profile,
      you are the controller and we are your processor, and the data processing terms in{' '}
      <a href="#data-processing">Schedule 1</a> apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="accounts">
    <h2>16. Your accounts and third-party platforms</h2>
    <p>
      16.1 Wherever a platform allows it, you will give us access by adding us as a user with
      appropriate permissions, rather than by sharing your password. You remain the owner of each
      account and can remove our access at any time. When our work ends, we will stop using our
      access and ask you to remove it.
    </p>
    <p>
      16.2 Google, your web host, your domain registrar and other platforms set their own rules,
      verification checks, rankings and policies, and can change or suspend services. We are not
      responsible for their decisions, outages or changes, or for any loss they cause, but we will
      help you respond where we can.
    </p>
    <p>
      16.3 A Google Business Profile is free to own. We charge for our work on it, not for the
      profile itself, and we are independent of Google.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="reviews">
    <h2>17. Reviews and listings</h2>
    <p>
      17.1 We only help businesses gather genuine reviews from real customers and respond to them
      honestly. We will not write, buy, sell or incentivise fake reviews, ask only satisfied
      customers for reviews in a way that misleads, hide genuine negative reviews, or post
      misleading business information. Fake reviews are banned under the Digital Markets,
      Competition and Consumers Act 2024, and we may stop work if asked to do any of these things.
    </p>
    <p>
      17.2 You are responsible for making sure the information you ask us to publish about your
      business is true and not misleading.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="liability">
    <h2>18. Our liability</h2>
    <h3>If you are a business customer</h3>
    <p>
      18.1 We are not liable for any loss of profit, revenue, sales, business, contracts, goodwill
      or anticipated savings, for any loss or corruption of data, or for any indirect or
      consequential loss, however it arises.
    </p>
    <p>
      18.2 Our total liability to you under or in connection with a contract, whether in contract,
      negligence or otherwise, is limited to the total fees you paid or must pay us under that
      contract in the 12 months before the event giving rise to the claim.
    </p>
    <p>
      18.3 We have set these limits because our prices are low compared with the value of the
      businesses we work for, and you are better placed to insure against business losses.
    </p>
    <h3>If you are a consumer</h3>
    <p>
      18.4 We are responsible for loss or damage you suffer that is a foreseeable result of our
      breaking these terms or failing to use reasonable care and skill. Loss is foreseeable if it is
      obvious that it will happen, or if we both knew it might happen when the contract was made.
      Because you hire us as a consumer and not for business purposes, we are not responsible for
      any loss of profit, business or business opportunity.
    </p>
    <h3>For everyone</h3>
    <p>18.5 Nothing in these terms limits or excludes our liability for:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>death or personal injury caused by our negligence;</li>
      <li>fraud or fraudulent misrepresentation;</li>
      <li>
        for consumers, breach of your rights under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, including that
        services will be performed with reasonable care and skill and as described; or
      </li>
      <li>anything else that cannot be limited or excluded by law.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="ending">
    <h2>19. Ending a contract</h2>
    <p>19.1 Either of us may end a contract immediately by written notice if the other:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        seriously breaks the contract and, if the breach can be put right, does not put it right
        within 14 days of being asked to in writing; or
      </li>
      <li>becomes insolvent, bankrupt or stops trading.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>19.2 When a contract ends, for whatever reason:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>you pay for work done and costs properly incurred up to the date it ends;</li>
      <li>
        once paid, we hand over the deliverables and any files, information and access details we
        hold for you;
      </li>
      <li>we stop using our access to your accounts; and</li>
      <li>
        we return or delete your personal information as Schedule 1 sets out, except where we must
        keep it by law.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      19.3 Clauses that are intended to continue after a contract ends, including those on payment,
      ownership, confidentiality, data protection and liability, continue to apply.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="outside-control">
    <h2>20. Events outside our control</h2>
    <p>
      20.1 Neither of us is responsible for a delay or failure caused by events outside our
      reasonable control, such as serious illness, extreme weather, a widespread internet or power
      outage, or a failure of a platform we rely on. The affected party will tell the other as soon
      as possible and take reasonable steps to limit the effect. If the delay lasts more than 30
      days, either of us may end the contract by written notice, and you pay only for work done.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="complaints">
    <h2>21. Complaints and disputes</h2>
    <p>
      21.1 If you are unhappy with our work or service, please tell us by email to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a>
      {business.telephone && ` or by phone on ${business.telephone}`}
      {business.correspondenceAddress && `, or by post to ${business.correspondenceAddress}`}. We
      will respond as quickly as possible, normally within five working days, and make our best
      efforts to find a fair solution.
    </p>
    <p>
      21.2 We are not a member of any alternative dispute resolution scheme and are not obliged to
      use one. If you are a consumer and we can’t resolve your complaint, you can get free advice
      from Consumerline (see{' '}
      <a
        href="https://www.nidirect.gov.uk/articles/about-consumerline-and-how-contact-helpline"
        rel="external"
      >
        nidirect
      </a>
      ), and you remain free to take your complaint to court.
    </p>
    <p>
      21.3 These terms, and any dispute or claim arising from them or the services, including
      non-contractual disputes, are governed by the law of Northern Ireland. The courts of Northern
      Ireland have exclusive jurisdiction. If you are a consumer living in England, Wales or
